# REVIEW 394f6a9 (fable_pc's P62 d): a sub called as a statement binds its named arguments like any call --
# add(b: 1; a: 8) gives a 8, b 1, and a - b is 7 (by position it would be -7).  Before the named actuals:
# "unknown method".  add keeps the difference in d, a field declared in its body -- the method's state,
# published when it returns (book §12) -- and the root reads it at the place of declaration, add\d.  (It used
# to write the unit's y bare: that changes only add's hidden input -- Q52, the author 2026-09-28.)  Entry 7.
sub: add (int: a; int: b)
    int: d 0
    d: a - b
end: add

add(b: 1; a: 8)
int: got 0
got: add\d
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: got; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
